# Mirebridge (Docs/Design/62-talk.md, section 3): the Sallow Fens by the Long River. Wry, damp, suspicious; mud,
# eels, reeds and mizzle. Drafts (phase 3), to be written properly with you (phase 7).

grammar mirebridge
greet = Dry feet | Dry feet to you
greet.broad = Dry feet, dry feet
farewell = Dry feet
agree = Fen-sure | Sure as mud
refuse = Boggy, that | Not on your life
surprise = Well, drown me | Sink me
annoyed = Mud and more mud | Eels take it
very = right
good = dry | fair-dry
bad = boggy | soggy
worthless = sump water
luck = a dry week
child = tadpole
pet!! = my reed | duck
rain = mizzle | murk
cold = damp | raw
nonsense = bog-wind
tag = , no?
oath! = Mud and eels | Sinking gods | Reed and rot
home = my own dry corner
# (Phase 7, the rest of plain's words.)
address = neighbour | fen-friend
address[other.stage=young] = tadpole | young 'un
bed = my dry corner | the reed-bed
dear! = my reed | my heron | duck
drunk = soused | sodden
hot = muggy | close
hungry = hollow | starved as a heron
hurt = sore | stiff as a reed
little = a dab | a drop
money = coin | eel-money
robbery = Eel-slippery robbery, that | Thieving, like a heron at the weir
saying! = Mud's mud | Never trust dry ground | It all goes back to the fen
thanks = Thank you kindly | Dry thanks
tired = sunk | bog-tired
work = the work | the eel-traps | the cutting
# (Phase 7, the conversations' words.)
wages = pay | eel-money
boss = the master | the old heron
busy = up to my knees in it | busy
slow = still as a pool | slow
cheated = eeled | done
mum! = Mam | Mother
dad! = Da | Father
gran = Granny | Old Mam
grandad = Grandda | Old Da
supper = supper | the eel-pot
toffs = the dry-footed lot | the toffs
skint = sunk | skint
posh = dry-footed | grand
busybody = reed-whisperer | gossip
fool = you mudskipper | you soggy lump
daft = bog-brained | daft
toast = Dry feet | Dry feet and full traps | Here's mud in your eye
drink = a jar | a drop of fen-gin
fog = fen-murk | mist | murk
mud = mud | slubber | ooze
wind = fen-wind | a blow
thief = eel-thief | sneak
bandits = marsh-robbers | road wolves
grave = the fen | the mud
grub = eels | grub
snug = dry | snug
poorly = damp in the chest | poorly
chill = the fen-ague | a chill
remedy = fen-gin | a mustard poultice | willow bark
olddays = the old floods | days gone by
blessing = Dry feet to you | Keep out of the water | Dry feet and luck
bless = Dry feet | The fen spare you
far = over the causeway and beyond | a long wade
stranger = dry-footer | stranger
